# Python (serve.py serves the published build)
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Model access tokens a creator pasted (#9). Never committed.
.sage/model-api-credentials.json
# Real rows from a Data Source, shown to the agent because the creator asked (#16). Never committed:
# the rest of .sage/ rides into the published app's container, which is right for column names and
# wrong for production data.
.sage/samples.json
# Chat-local files. They persist on this volume and do not travel with git.
.sage/scratch/
.sage/chat-work/
# A Thread record half-written when the container died. ThreadStore._write_json renames the real
# file into place, so one of these is never read — but `git add -A` would commit it.
.sage/threads/*/.*.tmp
# The agent-facing rendering of history.jsonl. Generated whole from the log beside it on every
# turn, so with two Sage Builders in one Project it conflicted every turn over data either one
# could rebuild. Ignored and regenerated on demand; the log itself stays committed. `.ignore` next
# to this file is what keeps the agent's grep able to see it.
.sage/history.md
# The stop button's private content store (TurnSnapshot). Builder-local scratch, like the two
# above. It is a git repo inside this one, so without this line `git add -A` records it as a
# gitlink pointing at a SHA no remote has, changing every turn, and a fresh clone gets an empty
# directory anyway. Ignoring it also keeps `git add -A` from failing outright in the window
# where the store exists with no commit yet.
.sage/snapshots/
# What the end-of-turn scan found the app's own source using, for the Build header's usage label
# (#93). Derived whole from the tree beside it every build turn, like `.sage/history.md` above, and
# written after that turn's commit — so committing it would conflict between two Sage Builders in
# one Project over an answer either one can rebuild, and leave a dirty file behind every turn.
.sage/usage.json
